---
// counterfeit/five-world-religions 1105 V3.astro
// V3 (Etsy Batch 1105 V1): the Buddhism card gains "Read the study: The Serpent King →",
// under the 38 Blessings link, in the same link style.
// V2 (Etsy Handoff 38 Blessings 1105 V2): the Buddhism card gains "Read the study:
// The 38 Blessings →" below its thread line. Nothing else changed.
// V1 (Etsy Handoff Counterfeit 1102 V1): the landing page of the Counterfeit
// section, from Five World Religions 1102 V3.html, text as written. Wrapped in
// BaseLayout (its footer replaces the page's own); the page palette moved from
// :root/body onto .fwr, and classes carry an fwr- prefix so the site's global
// .card and heading rules cannot reach them. Styles stay scoped (nothing here is
// built at runtime).
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Five Major World Religions | Nazaryah"
  description="Hinduism, Buddhism, Rabbinic Judaism, Islam, and Christianity, each set beside Scripture: what it kept of the truth, where it came from, and where it turned sideways."
>
  <div class="fwr">
  <div class="fwr-wrap">


  <p class="fwr-kicker">Counterfeit</p>
  <h1>The Five Major World Religions</h1>
  <p class="fwr-deck">Truth kept, then turned sideways.</p>

  <section class="fwr-intro">
    <p>A counterfeit only works when it looks like the real thing. No one prints a bill that resembles nothing; the closer the copy runs to the genuine, the easier it spends. The five largest religions on earth each hold pieces of truth that trace back to the Word, and each one turns those pieces sideways.</p>
    <p>Scripture explains why the pieces are there at all. Every nation descends from the sons of Noah (Genesis 10:32; Acts 17:26), and every nation once knew the Creator. Paul says they knew Him, yet "glorified him not as God" and traded His glory for images (Romans 1:21-23). What remained was a memory of the truth with the Root cut away. Even then, the work of the Law stayed written in the conscience (Romans 2:14-15), which is why so much of what these religions teach sounds familiar.</p>
    <p>Each study in this section takes one religion on its own terms, from its own writings, and sets it beside Scripture: what it kept, where it came from, and where it turned. Christianity stands last on purpose. By the time a reader reaches it, the pattern will already be familiar.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="fwr-grid">

    <!-- HINDUISM -->
    <article class="fwr-card">
      <svg class="fwr-symbol" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-label="Om symbol">
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="47" fill="none" stroke="#B8902F" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="50" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="54" fill="#1F3864"
          font-family="'Noto Sans Devanagari','Kohinoor Devanagari','Devanagari Sangam MN','Mangal',sans-serif">&#x0950;</text>
      </svg>
      <h2>Hinduism</h2>
      <ul class="fwr-facts">
        <li><b>Began:</b> no single founder; roots in the Vedic hymns of northern India, about 1500 BC</li>
        <li><b>Followers:</b> about 1.15 billion</li>
        <li><b>Writings:</b> the Vedas, the Upanishads, the Bhagavad Gita</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Hinduism is the oldest of the five and the hardest to pin down, more a family of traditions than a single church. Most Hindus hold that behind everything stands one ultimate reality, Brahman, which shows itself through many gods and goddesses such as Vishnu, Shiva, and Devi. Life is a wheel of birth, death, and rebirth (<i>samsara</i>), driven by the weight of a person's deeds (<i>karma</i>). The goal is release from the wheel (<i>moksha</i>).</p>
      <h3>The Symbol</h3>
      <p>Om (Aum), the sacred syllable chanted at the opening of prayers and readings, held to be the sound of ultimate reality itself.</p>
      <p class="fwr-thread">The thread to follow: one Source behind all things and a moral order, split into many gods, carved images, and endless rebirth.</p>
    </article>

    <!-- BUDDHISM -->
    <article class="fwr-card">
      <svg class="fwr-symbol" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-label="Dharma wheel">
        <g fill="none" stroke="#1F3864">
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="44" stroke-width="5"/>
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="37" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="#B8902F"/>
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="11" stroke-width="4"/>
          <g stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round">
            <line x1="50" y1="39" x2="50" y2="8"/>
            <line x1="50" y1="61" x2="50" y2="92"/>
            <line x1="39" y1="50" x2="8" y2="50"/>
            <line x1="61" y1="50" x2="92" y2="50"/>
            <line x1="57.8" y1="42.2" x2="79.7" y2="20.3"/>
            <line x1="42.2" y1="57.8" x2="20.3" y2="79.7"/>
            <line x1="42.2" y1="42.2" x2="20.3" y2="20.3"/>
            <line x1="57.8" y1="57.8" x2="79.7" y2="79.7"/>
          </g>
        </g>
        <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="4" fill="#B8902F"/>
      </svg>
      <h2>Buddhism</h2>
      <ul class="fwr-facts">
        <li><b>Began:</b> Siddhartha Gautama, called the Buddha, northeast India, about the 5th century BC</li>
        <li><b>Followers:</b> about 536 million</li>
        <li><b>Writings:</b> the Pali Canon (Tipitaka) and later Mahayana sutras</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Buddhism grew out of one man's search for an end to suffering. The Buddha taught the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering, suffering comes from craving, craving can be ended, and the Eightfold Path is the way to end it. Buddhism names no Creator. The goal is Nirvana, the "blowing out" of craving and freedom from rebirth. It is the national faith of Thailand, Sri Lanka, Myanmar, and Cambodia.</p>
      <h3>The Symbol</h3>
      <p>The Dharma wheel (<i>Dharmachakra</i>). Its eight spokes stand for the Eightfold Path, and its turning stands for the Buddha setting his teaching in motion.</p>
      <p class="fwr-thread">The thread to follow: sound moral conduct, as in the 38 Blessings of the Mangala Sutta, with the Creator removed and stored-up merit put in the place of favor.</p>
      <p class="fwr-study"><a class="fwr-growth-link" href="/counterfeit/buddhism/38-blessings">Read the study: The 38 Blessings →</a></p>
      <p class="fwr-study"><a class="fwr-growth-link" href="/counterfeit/the-serpent-king">Read the study: The Serpent King →</a></p>
    </article>

    <!-- RABBINIC JUDAISM -->
    <article class="fwr-card">
      <svg class="fwr-symbol" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-label="Six-pointed star">
        <g fill="none" stroke="#1F3864" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <polygon points="50,8 86.4,71 13.6,71"/>
          <polygon points="50,92 13.6,29 86.4,29"/>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <h2>Rabbinic Judaism</h2>
      <ul class="fwr-facts">
        <li><b>Began:</b> rebuilt after the Temple fell in AD 70; Mishnah about AD 200; Babylonian Talmud about AD 500</li>
        <li><b>Followers:</b> about 16 million</li>
        <li><b>Writings:</b> the Hebrew Scriptures, read through the Mishnah and Talmud</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Judaism as practiced today is not the faith of Moses carried forward unchanged. After the Temple was destroyed in AD 70, the rabbis rebuilt the religion around the synagogue and an "oral law" they hold was handed down from Sinai beside the written Torah, later set down in the Mishnah and Talmud. It keeps the Sabbath, the feasts, and the Hebrew Scriptures, but reads them through rabbinic rulings. It does not receive Yahushua as Messiah, and the Name Yahuah is not spoken; a title is said in its place.</p>
      <h3>The Symbol</h3>
      <p>The six-pointed star (<i>Magen David</i>). It appears nowhere in the Torah and became the common emblem of the Jewish people only in recent centuries. The symbol Scripture gives is the lampstand (Exodus 25:31-40).</p>
      <p class="fwr-thread">The thread to follow: the Torah, the Sabbath, the feasts, and the Hebrew Scriptures, buried under the traditions of men (Mark 7:7-9) and the Messiah refused.</p>
    </article>

    <!-- ISLAM -->
    <article class="fwr-card">
      <svg class="fwr-symbol" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-label="Star and crescent">
        <defs>
          <mask id="fwr-crescent">
            <rect width="100" height="100" fill="#fff"/>
            <circle cx="55" cy="46" r="31" fill="#000"/>
          </mask>
        </defs>
        <circle cx="44" cy="50" r="38" fill="#1F3864" mask="url(#fwr-crescent)"/>
        <polygon fill="#B8902F" points="74,32 77.4,42.6 88.5,42.6 79.5,49.2 82.9,59.8 74,53.2 65.1,59.8 68.5,49.2 59.5,42.6 70.6,42.6"/>
      </svg>
      <h2>Islam</h2>
      <ul class="fwr-facts">
        <li><b>Began:</b> Muhammad, Arabia, from AD 610</li>
        <li><b>Followers:</b> about 2.1 billion</li>
        <li><b>Writings:</b> the Quran, with the Hadith</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Islam teaches strict belief in one God, called Allah, and submission to his will; the word <i>Islam</i> means submission. It honors Abraham, Moses, David, and Jesus (Isa) as prophets, with Muhammad as the last and greatest. The Quran denies that God has a son and denies that Jesus was crucified, teaching that it only appeared so (Surah 4:157). Daily practice rests on the Five Pillars: the confession, prayer five times a day, almsgiving, the fast of Ramadan, and the pilgrimage to Mecca.</p>
      <h3>The Symbol</h3>
      <p>The star and crescent. It is older than Islam, does not come from the Quran, and became tied to the faith through the Ottoman Turks. Many Muslims reject any symbol for their religion.</p>
      <p class="fwr-thread">The thread to follow: one God, Abraham, Moses, and many commandments, with the Son denied, the cross denied, and acceptance earned by deeds weighed on a scale.</p>
    </article>

    <!-- CHRISTIANITY -->
    <article class="fwr-card">
      <svg class="fwr-symbol" viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-label="Cross">
        <rect x="44" y="8" width="12" height="84" rx="1.5" fill="#1F3864"/>
        <rect x="22" y="28" width="56" height="12" rx="1.5" fill="#1F3864"/>
      </svg>
      <h2>Christianity</h2>
      <ul class="fwr-facts">
        <li><b>Began:</b> rooted in Yahushua and His apostles, 1st century AD; its creeds fixed at the councils of Nicaea (AD 325) and Constantinople (AD 381)</li>
        <li><b>Followers:</b> about 2.7 billion</li>
        <li><b>Writings:</b> the Bible, read through the creeds</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Christianity is the largest religion on earth and claims to worship the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob through Yahushua the Messiah. It holds the Scriptures, the virgin birth, the cross, and the resurrection. Yet the faith most Christians practice was shaped less by the apostles than by the centuries that followed them: a Trinity of three persons, the first day of the week in place of the Sabbath, Rome's holidays in place of Yahuah's feasts, and His Law declared finished.</p>
      <h3>The Symbol</h3>
      <p>The cross. Early believers more often marked themselves with a fish or an anchor; the cross came into common use as the emblem of the faith from the fourth century on, after Constantine.</p>
      <p class="fwr-thread">The thread to follow: the Messiah, His blood, and the Scriptures themselves, wrapped in a Trinity, a Roman calendar, and a Law set aside.</p>
    </article>

  </section>

  <p class="fwr-close"><a class="fwr-growth-link" href="/counterfeit/five-world-religions/growth">How They Have Grown →</a><br><span class="fwr-note">Follower counts are for 2026 (World Christian Database). The growth page sets each religion against the growth of the world since 1900.</span></p>

  <p class="fwr-close">Revelation names the people who come through every counterfeit standing: those who keep the commandments of Yahuah and hold to the belief of Yahushua (Revelation 14:12). Nothing kept back, and nothing turned sideways.</p>
  </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fwr {
    --navy: #1F3864;
    --ink: #222;
    --muted: #5a5f6b;
    --gold: #B8902F;
    --paper: #fdfcf8;
    --card: #ffffff;
    --line: #e4e0d4;
  }
  .fwr *, .fwr *::before, .fwr *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
  .fwr {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  /* The site's global headings are Cinzel; this page's headings keep its own serif. */
  .fwr h1, .fwr h2, .fwr h3 { font-family: inherit; }
  .fwr-wrap { max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 16px 24px; }
  .fwr-kicker {
    font-variant: small-caps;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
    color: var(--gold);
    font-size: .95rem;
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .fwr h1 { color: var(--navy); font-size: 2.2rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 6px; font-weight: normal; }
  .fwr-deck { font-style: italic; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0 0 28px; }
  .fwr-intro p { margin: 0 0 14px; font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 760px; }
  .fwr-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: 22px;
    margin-top: 34px;
  }
  .fwr-card {
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-top: 4px solid var(--navy);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 24px 22px 22px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .fwr-symbol { width: 96px; height: 96px; margin: 0 auto 14px; display: block; }
  .fwr-card h2 { color: var(--navy); text-align: center; font-weight: normal; font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .fwr-facts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 14px; font-size: .92rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .fwr-facts li { margin: 0 0 4px; }
  .fwr-facts b { color: var(--navy); font-weight: normal; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: .05em; }
  .fwr-card p { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 1rem; }
  .fwr-card h3 { color: var(--navy); font-size: 1rem; font-weight: normal; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: .06em; margin: 8px 0 4px; }
  .fwr-thread { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 10px; font-style: italic; }
    .fwr-growth-link { color: var(--navy); text-decoration: none; font-size: 1.15rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gold); }
  .fwr-card .fwr-study { margin: 6px 0 0; }
  .fwr-note { font-size: .92rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .fwr-close { max-width: 760px; margin: 40px 0 0; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  @media (max-width: 480px) { .fwr h1 { font-size: 1.8rem; } }
</style>
